#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088668} :activity/id #uuid "c5111638-8050-5ca1-a687-ed434d6556aa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049182 :topic/id #uuid "e1819049-d5ad-572e-bf0c-2abb071dc9a4" :topic/math-academy-id 885} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088851 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x) = e^{3x}.$ Computing this using the formula is a lot of work because we have to calculate many derivatives and then plug them into the formula. Is there any easier way?\n\nYes, there is! First, let's recall the Maclaurin expansion of $e^x{:}$\n\n$$\ne^x = 1 + x + \\dfrac{x^2}{2!} + \\dfrac{x^3}{3!} + \\cdots, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nSimply replacing $x$ with $3x$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x} & =1+(3x)+\\frac{(3x)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(3x)^{3}}{3!}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+3x+\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{27{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+3x+\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the new interval of convergence, we do the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & \\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\ -\\infty  & <3x<\\infty  \\\\ -\\infty  & <x<\\infty  \\\\ x & \\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the interval of convergence stays the same, and we have\n\n$$\ne^{3x} = 1 + 3x + \\dfrac{9x^2}{2} + \\dfrac{9x^3}{2}  + \\cdots, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! Notice how much quicker this was compared with calculating the expansion from scratch." :tutorial/id #uuid "96030fee-2ce6-5a54-86a7-0e9c64d26bc7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1307 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f4da26a5-2471-58ea-91c9-3d394365dd82" :step/math-academy-id 2570 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087787 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087633 :question/id #uuid "895adb0f-347a-52e1-a160-3fbba31a4c49" :question/math-academy-id "e-754"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd566f7-15e2-59d9-bf6a-1fa3598399b9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050812}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404184}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Composite Function Using a Standard Maclaurin Expansion"} :step/id #uuid "bf07391e-3460-58b6-90e7-6c1b023dcdc0" :step/math-academy-id 2571 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088694}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087788 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087634 :question/id #uuid "7f8c3610-ab9a-5bee-bcc5-a817a79b50cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-755"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffddeaa7-a247-54b0-af4b-a29d9bbeee6a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389284}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Sum of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions"} :step/id #uuid "668d6691-289b-5210-b9fc-1aafe63d434c" :step/math-academy-id 2574 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088671}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087789 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087635 :question/id #uuid "e29f6eb6-9b2a-5f03-aad3-3bc36dbe400e" :question/math-academy-id "e-756"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b839f19a-161c-5cb4-bc08-990f09afb900" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050734}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387650}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Logarithmic Function"} :step/id #uuid "71fa7742-aee9-5fb7-8556-20e4c6b386ba" :step/math-academy-id 2576 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087790 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087636 :question/id #uuid "f4e495eb-3712-5106-abce-ab07f15fb328" :question/math-academy-id "e-757"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de71fcb6-36ca-581a-92ea-47c1d0c6bf10" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049500}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404245}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Product of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions"} :step/id #uuid "24d27f91-863a-5781-8829-9273038dd369" :step/math-academy-id 2578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088968 :tutorial/content "In order to quickly compute the Maclaurin expansions of a variety of functions, it's worth memorizing some of the most basic Maclaurin expansions so that you can use them as starting points. These are summarized below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x}=1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}{x}^{3}+⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\operatorname{sin}(x)=x-\\frac{1}{3!}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{5!}{x}^{5}-⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\operatorname{cos}(x)=1-\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}{x}^{4}-⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\operatorname{ln}(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-1,1] \\\\  & \\frac{1}{1-x}=1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-1,1) \\\\  & \\frac{1}{1+x}=1-x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+⋯ &  & x\\in (-1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To memorize the Maclaurin expansions above, it's helpful to notice the following:\n\n- The series for $\\dfrac{1}{1-x}$ consists a geometric series whose first term is $1$ and whose common ratio is $x.$ This makes sense, because the sum formula for a geometric series with first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r$ is $\\dfrac{a_1}{1-r}.$\n\n- The series for $\\sin (x)$ consists of the alternating sum of the odd-degree terms in the series for $e^x.$ This makes sense because $\\sin(x)$ is an odd function (its graph is symmetric across the origin).\n\n- The series for $\\cos (x)$ consists of the alternating sum of the even-degree terms in the series for $e^x.$ This makes sense because $\\cos(x)$ is an even function (its graph is symmetric across the $y$-axis).\n\n- The series for $e^x,$ $\\sin(x),$ and $\\cos(x)$ all have a factorial in the denominator and no factorial in the numerator. For all of our standard series, any term with a factorial in the denominator will converge for all $x,$ because the factorial grows much more quickly than the powers of $x.$ So, their intervals of convergence are $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1801e6eb-5326-5b15-a38c-b91f970d7a15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4612 :tutorial/title "The Basic Maclaurin Expansions"} :step/id #uuid "5dc233c4-4b38-5913-8b2d-1f2421ba65d7" :step/math-academy-id 28401 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088969 :tutorial/content "To find the Maclaurin expansion for a sum, difference, or product of functions, we can\n\n- find the Maclaurin expansions of the individual functions, and then\n\n- perform the corresponding operation on the expansions.\n\nThe only catch is that we must take the intersection of the intervals of convergence, since we want to find where *both* of the individual functions converge.\n\nFor example, recall the following two Maclaurin expansions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x}=1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}{x}^{3}+⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\frac{1}{1-x}=1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the Maclaurin expansion of $e^x+\\dfrac{1}{1-x},$ we can simply add the two expansions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{1-x} & =(1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}{x}^{3}+⋯)+(1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯) \\\\  & =(1+1)+(1+1)x+(\\frac{1}{2!}+1){x}^{2}+(\\frac{1}{3!}+1){x}^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =2+2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the interval of convergence, we take the intersection of the intervals of convergence of the individual functions:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, \\infty) \\cap (-1, 1) = (-1, 1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{1-x}=2+2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{3}+⋯,\\, &  & x\\in (-1,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "29e51064-92e4-53b6-870b-05fec1aa937f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4613 :tutorial/title "The Maclaurin Expansion of a Sum, Difference, or Product of Functions"} :step/id #uuid "a2fb2ac1-05c2-545a-b8fc-870f2fe95150" :step/math-academy-id 28402 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088670}}] :activity/title "Representing Functions as Power Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087950}]}
